    "title": "New Universal Studios roller coaster goes full throttle with real people onboard",
    "text": "by: Marc Sternfield Posted: Apr 10, 2026 / 02:18 PM PDT Updated: Apr 11, 2026 / 06:19 AM PDT The first known instance of people, not test dummies, taking a spin on the new Fast & Furious roller coaster at Universal Studios Hollywood happened on Friday. TikTok user @Cykrolis livestreamed the ride in full testing mode, showing several people, presumably members of the development team, riding in two of the cars as it sped along the 4,100-foot track. “They look like they’re having a blast!” he said during the stream. “Those are not dummies. Those are real, breathing people.” Univeral Creative Vice President Jon Corfino was also among those who went for a “drive” on the new ride. Fast & Furious: Hollywood Drift, Universal Studios Hollywood’s first high-speed outdoor coaster, reaches speeds of up to 72 miles per hour as it races through a series of launches and inversions from the park’s upper lot to the lower lot and back again. Human riders are seen on Fast & Furious: Hollywood Drift at Universal Studios Hollywood on April 10, 2026. (TikTok: @cykrolis_) The cars spin independently, replicating the drifting action of race scenes from the Fast & Furious movie franchise. Because the park sits just a short distance from residential areas in Studio City and Toluca Lake, designers have taken steps to keep riders’ screams contained. Two half-pipe-style sound shields have been installed along the coaster’s main loops, one at the western end of the ride and another above the Starway Escalator near the finale. The coaster is slated to open this summer, though a tentative opening date has not yet been announced.",
